+# Agent Note: LSP action operations join the closed ctx.lsp union
+
+Status: implemented
+
+## Problem
+
+The LSP seam exposes four read-only navigation operations (`goToDefinition`, `findReferences`, `goToImplementation`, `hover`). Diagnostics, formatting, and completion are language-server capabilities an agent also needs, but each has a different request and result shape: diagnostics is file-scoped with no cursor, formatting takes an optional range and returns edits, and completion returns a list. Without them in the seam, a consumer would need a parallel service or escape hatch, fragmenting the closed vocabulary and re-opening the arbitrary-JSON-RPC path the seam exists to prevent.
+
+## Decision
+
+Extend the closed `LspOperation` union from four to seven operations — adding `diagnostics`, `formatDocument`, and `completion` — so the compile-enforced ripple reaches every new operation. Split `LspQueryRequest` into a closed union of three request shapes: position-based (`LspPositionRequest`), position-less (`LspDiagnosticsRequest`), and optional-range (`LspFormatRequest`). Extend `LspQueryResult` from two kinds to five: `locations`, `hover`, `diagnostics`, `edits`, and `completion`.
+
+## Why actions join the closed union
+
+The closed union is the seam's negative guarantee: no arbitrary JSON-RPC method reaches `ctx.lsp`, and adding an operation is a compile-enforced change across the Service Definition, every provider, and every consumer. Diagnostics, formatting, and completion belong inside that union rather than a separate escape hatch, so a provider translates them into the normalized request and result contracts and every consumer `switch` handles them. A future eighth operation breaks every exhaustive switch until handled — the same guarantee the four navigation operations already enforce.
+
+## Why the request became a discriminated union
+
+The four navigation operations share one request shape (`operation` + `filePath` + `position` + `workspaceRoot`). The three new operations do not: `diagnostics` has no cursor, and `formatDocument` carries an optional range instead of a required position. One flat request with optional fields would reintroduce hidden defaulting and let a caller send fields an operation ignores. The discriminated union makes each shape exact: `operation` narrows `LspQueryRequest` to the fields that operation actually carries, so `request.position` is reachable only for position-based operations and `request.range` only for `formatDocument`.
+
+## Why diagnostics has a push fallback path
+
+Pull diagnostics (`textDocument/diagnostic`) is optional in the protocol; many servers only push `textDocument/publishDiagnostics` after a document opens. A server that advertises `diagnosticProvider` is queried by pull; a server without it is queried by the transient-open push path: open the document, wait up to a bounded settle window for a `publishDiagnostics` for that URI (the latest batch replaces earlier batches for the same URI), then close. The settle window exists because push delivery has no completion signal — the client cannot know a silent server has nothing to report, so it bounds the wait.
+
+Pushed URIs are matched by normalization, not raw string equality: the client percent-decodes the pathname and case-folds it on Windows, because a server may re-spell the opened document URI (typescript-language-server percent-encodes the drive colon and lowercases it, turning `file:///C:/…` into `file:///c%3A/…`). The default `diagnosticsSettleMs` of `2000` bounds the wait but can miss a cold-start push: typescript-language-server pushes diagnostics roughly `2.4s` after `didOpen`, after a `workspace/configuration` round trip, so a warm server pushes fast while a cold server may land just past the default window. Deployments whose servers start slowly can raise `diagnosticsSettleMs`.
+
+## Why formatting returns edits and the host never applies them
+
+`formatDocument` normalizes to `edits` (`LspTextEdit[]`), not to a mutated document. The stdio host transiently opens the document for the query and applies nothing: it rejects `workspace/applyEdit` and never writes. Applying an edit is a mutation with preview, permission, and write-policy obligations the query seam does not own. A consumer applies the returned edits through `ctx.fs` write-intent, so the query itself stays read-only for the host.
+
+## Keyless-snapshot judgment
+
+No keyless snapshot is added. The in-repo model-visible surface is unchanged: `tool-lsp` still exposes the same four navigation operations and the same `locations`/`hover` output schema, and its switches throw on the three new result kinds it never requests. The new seam and provider additions surface only through the external `dsh-lsp-actions` consumer plugin, which owns its own model-facing schema and snapshots.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Add a parallel diagnostics/format/completion service or escape hatch.** Rejected: it would fragment the closed vocabulary and re-open the arbitrary-JSON-RPC path the seam exists to close.
+
+**Keep one flat request with optional fields.** Rejected: optional fields reintroduce hidden defaulting and let a caller send fields an operation ignores; the discriminated union makes each operation's fields exact.
+
+**Make diagnostics pull-only.** Rejected: push-only servers are common, and the transient-open sequence already exists, so a bounded push fallback serves them without new infrastructure.
+
+**Apply formatting edits inside the provider.** Rejected: mutation needs preview, permission, and write-policy integration the query seam does not own; returning edits keeps the query read-only and leaves application to the consumer through `ctx.fs`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The closed union now spans seven operations, so a new provider implements — or rejects with `LSP_UNSUPPORTED_OPERATION` — all of them, and the compile-enforced switches keep that honest. `formatDocument` returns edits the host never applies, so a consumer that wants formatted text writes it back through `ctx.fs`. Push diagnostics are bounded by `diagnosticsSettleMs`, so a silent server resolves to an empty list after the window rather than hanging. The model-facing `lsp` tool is unchanged, so the seam additions reach no model request until a consumer plugin exposes them.
